# /.agents/ — machine-local, auto-generated `.claude`->Codex mirror of the canonical tracked
# `.claude/skills/`. It is NOT the source of truth (that is `.claude/skills/`, committed) and it is
# currently broken: its SKILL.md files instruct `node .Codex/skills/.../scripts/*.mjs`, a path that
# does not exist (the real scripts live under `.agents/skills/.../scripts/`) — a naive find-replace
# rewrote the prose but not the file layout. Ignored (not committed: would double-track + bless broken
# paths) purely so the broken mirror does not dirty the build stamp.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED (`/.agents/`, corrective follow-up 2026-07-20): a bare `.agents/` matches a dir of
# that name at ANY depth, so it could silently hide a real nested project dir that happens to be
# named `.agents`. The leading slash pins the ignore to the repo-root mirror ONLY.
#
# gitignore IS NOT A SKILL-DISABLE MECHANISM: ignoring this dir only keeps it OUT OF GIT — the files
# stay on disk and Codex still DISCOVERS and can load these skills at runtime (Codex reads
# `.codex/`/`.agents/` from the filesystem, not from git's index). So this entry does NOT neutralize
# the broken mirror for a Codex session; it only stops git from tracking it. To actually stop Codex
# from using it, the dir must be QUARANTINED or DELETED on disk — that is an OWNER decision (it is
# generated, non-obvious, and irreversible without a regenerate step), and it must NOT be regenerated
# until a Codex-native copy is intentionally designed. The reviewable, hand-authored Codex scaffold
# that IS committed and IS the intended source is `AGENTS.md` + `.codex/`.
/.agents/

# Beta distribution kit — locally-assembled artifacts (installer copy, README, invite) for handing
# to testers. Built on demand from committed sources; never committed.
beta-kit/
business/

# Compy Pro signing keys — the private license-signing key NEVER enters the repo (license-tool.mjs keygen
# writes it here / there). Defense-in-depth so it can't be committed by accident.
secrets/
*.key
compy-license.key

# Weekly usage-audit reports (machine-local, auto-written by the CompyUsageAudit scheduled task)
docs/usage-audit/
